The LHC first circulated a proton beam around its full 27 km ring on **10 September 2008**, during the initial commissioning of the machine. Nine days later, on 19 September 2008, an incident involving faulty electrical connections (a magnet quench caused by a superconducting joint failure) led to a helium leak and damage to dozens of magnets, halting operations for over a year. After repairs and re-commissioning, beams circulated again in November 2009, and first proton–proton collisions occurred on 23 November 2009.

So the earlier answer was wrong: the first full-ring beam circulation was in 2008, not 2009.

